    {
      "number": 1036,
      "title": "opencode: report the tokens the provider measured, not zeros",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow --lib harness::\n```\n\nThe two tests that carry the fix:\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow opencode_trace_reports_the_tokens_the_provider_measured\ncargo test -p loopflow opencode_trace_normal_turn_reports_no_usage_when_none_was_reported\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nopencode's token usage was being parsed correctly and then destroyed. `opencode_mapping::complete_turn` pushed a `TurnUsage` on *every* turn close, defaulting to zeros when the provider reported nothing — and `trace.rs` treats a reported `TurnUsage` as authoritative, clobbering the totals it had accumulated from the stream rather than merging them. Every opencode turn therefore ended by overwriting real token counts with \"the provider measured zero.\"\n\nThe fix is to only emit `TurnUsage` when usage was actually reported. Absent usage now means absent event, and the stream's accumulated totals stand. The same correction applies to the three paths that close a turn without any usage in hand: orphaned-turn cleanup on disconnect, session errors, and hollow-body closes — none of those reported a reading, so none of them invent one.\n\nThis started as a design task to retire one of the two parallel spend recorders. Measuring the live ledger inverted the received story: opencode was never missing from `agent_turns` (8 launches, 8 turns, usage parsed fine) — it was being zeroed at the mapping layer. That made this a one-line fix at the source rather than a new capture path, so it ships ahead of the rest of the design.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `opencode_mapping.rs`: emit `TurnUsage` only for reported usage; drop the defaulted event from `complete_turn`, `close_orphaned_turn`, session-error and hollow-body closes\n- New conformance fixture `opencode_reported_usage.ndjson` with a real idle-status usage payload, and a test asserting the values survive the mapping (40 in / 5,197 out / $0.985363 / `opencode/glm-5.2`)\n- Renamed `opencode_trace_normal_turn` to say what it pins — its fixture reports no usage, so it is the test that goes red against the defect; the value-asserting sibling stays green either way. Both are needed; the doc comment says why.\n- Design docs under `scratch/` recording the measured ledger state and the three findings that reshaped the task, including the open questions log (Session spend is recorded in *neither* store, and `run_events` misattributes provider on multi-launch processes)",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 61,
      "deletions": 46,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
