    {
      "number": 1049,
      "title": "lfd: bound daemon log storage",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlfd install --repo /path/to/repo\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nKeep daemon logs from becoming another source of disk pressure. `lfd` now owns a bounded 8 MiB current log plus one predecessor, while launchd and systemd stop duplicating output into unbounded service files.\n\n## Changes\n\n- rotate `lfd.log` at 8 MiB and retain one predecessor\n- honor `LF_HOME` for the log location\n- send launchd/systemd stdout and stderr to the null sink\n- cover rotation and service rendering with tests\n\n## Verification\n\n- `cargo test -p loopflow --bin lfd daemon_log_keeps_only_one_bounded_predecessor`\n- `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings`\n- `cargo fmt --all -- --check`",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 161,
      "deletions": 25,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 1050,
      "title": "task: fail closed on committed follow-up past the merged tip",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow ops::task\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nWhen a worker commits follow-up work on a settled branch after its PR merged, that work belongs to no PR. Previously a completing PR (`after_merge: CompleteTask`) would settle the Task the moment GitHub reported the merge, silently stranding those commits outside the Task. This makes the follow-up boundary an explicit, provable fact: completion is withheld while work sits past the merged tip, and the completing PR — normally terminal — rotates once more so the carry can land on a successor branch.\n\nThe helper that computed the carry range used to collapse \"nothing to carry\" and \"can't tell\" into a single `None`. That's fine for rotation (skip an unsafe cherry-pick) but wrong for completion (an unknown boundary is not an empty one). It now returns a three-way `CommittedFollowUp` — `ProvenEmpty`, `Range`, or `Unprovable` with a reason — so each caller decides for itself.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `committed_follow_up_range` returns `CommittedFollowUp` instead of `Option<(String, String)>`; a missing published head or a head that isn't an ancestor of the branch is `Unprovable`, not empty.\n- The completion gate inspects the newest PR only (rotation leaves the settled branch's commits in place, so scanning every merged PR would never clear) and blocks on a proven range or an unprovable boundary.\n- `Unprovable` does not reverse a Task already `Completed` — missing later evidence blocks entry into completion but can't undo a terminal fact. Repair still reopens on a proven range or any other concrete blocker.\n- Merge reconciliation withholds completion over a committed carry, reading the range from the in-flight PR rather than the store.\n- A completing PR now rotates in exactly one case: a proven `Range` to carry forward.\n- Tests cover rotation-to-carry, both unprovable shapes at the gate, and in-flight merge observation; the `gh` stubs now report a real branch head so fixtures reflect an authoritative merged tip.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 307,
      "deletions": 25,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 1051,
      "title": "task: persist existing headless interaction policy",
      "body": "",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 175,
      "deletions": 2,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 1053,
      "title": "Accounts become the routing primitive",
      "body": "Routes now order provider accounts directly, with a store-wide default for unrouted repositories. Chrome profiles remain only as verified login venues owned by accounts.\n\nThis PR migrates the store to account-first routes and session pins, rekeys forwarded credentials, adds account-addressed route/auth/profile commands, and refuses provider identity mismatches before installing credentials. Automatic runtime selection is SQLite-only; explicit --account selection retains live authentication verification.\n\nThe amended Gate proof runs through connect_account: a drifted Chrome login is skipped with both actual and expected identities rendered, then the next configured venue succeeds. The helper-only attempt path remains deleted. Sabotage control: deleting the expected-login guard makes Profile 3 open, so both the opened-profile assertion and the rendered drift-failure assertion fail; the guard is restored in the published head.\n\nExplicit account identity now composes with foreground SSH independent of repository routing. ForwardedAccountBundle has one required selection authority: routed selections carry a required repository id and routes, while pinned selections carry only concrete provider/account pins. lf --account engineering ssh mini -- lf -m codex : \"work here\" resolves and live-verifies the local account before repository discovery, forwards only its concrete pin and access token, and selects it remotely without a native home, route membership, or automatic health gating. A valid explicit account reaches foreground SSH from outside any repository; missing, ambiguous, empty, and unauthenticated selectors still fail before transport. No-flag SSH retains ordered route failover, and the foreground SSH path removes its temporary store and lease directory on exit.\n\nPR 3 remains intentionally separate: persisted limit-window demotion, route show --explain, and strained-account presentation.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 3049,
      "deletions": 2438,
      "changed_files": 19
    },
    {
      "number": 1054,
      "title": "task: wait for green CI before review handoff",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow ci_fix_lifecycle\ncargo test -p loopflow next_move\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAn open PR's next move is now always CI-derived until a fresh required-check reading for the current head proves the checks passed. Previously, a task with an open PR and no CI observation fell straight through to `NextMoveOwner::Review`, so a PR could be handed to review before anyone had seen its checks — including PRs whose checks were red or still running. Now the absence of a reading is itself an answer (\"required checks have not been observed for the current head\"), and CI keeps the move until it can prove otherwise.\n\nThe second half closes the loop on repair: project supervision reconciles its own tasks before touching bodies, so an idle task under a failing head gets a ci-fix wake armed from live control rather than waiting on the body to notice. Task runners consume or retain every ci-fix wake before the shared absorber in `child_control`, which narrows that fallback to the one real case — a child without a bounded repair path already owns the work — and the comment there now says so instead of describing the old birth-time race.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `waves.rs`: `next_move_for_task` returns `Ci` for an open PR with no fresh current-head reading; review requires observed-passing checks\n- `ops/task.rs`: new `reconcile_project_tasks` runs PR/liveness/completion reconciliation and queues ci-fix (or relaunches a parked gate body) ahead of `supervise_project_task_bodies`\n- `task/runner.rs` + `actions.rs`: idle runners arm the ci-fix wake; provider-owned turns retain the claim until the turn ends\n- Attention-projection test fixture now takes a `NextMove` and a CI observation directly, so the gate under test is the real one\n- Expanded `ci_fix_lifecycle_tests` with a parked-gate fixture covering green, failing, and unobserved heads\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1241,
      "deletions": 203,
      "changed_files": 8
    },
    {
      "number": 1055,
      "title": "release a body lease stuck at `revoked` when its body is provably gone",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow release\ncargo test -p loopflow probe\n```\n\nA Task or Project whose reap failed leaves its body lease pinned at `revoked`. Nothing in the system ever revisited that lease, and the reserve CAS accepts only `NULL` or `finished` — so every future generation was barred forever, and a resume was permanently refused by a corpse. This adds a fail-closed presence probe and releases the lease when the body can be *proven* gone.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe release turns on positive evidence only. `probe_child_body_presence` answers `Absent`, `Present`, or `Unprovable`, and only `Absent` releases; anything else leaves the lease exactly as it was, still doing its job. That third arm is the whole point of not reusing the existing helpers: `tmux_session_exists` maps any non-zero exit to `Ok(false)`, and `process_target_exists` folds `ESRCH` and every unmodelled errno into the same `false`. Both are fail-open, which is harmless for a bounded waiter and wrong for a release — reading an unanswered question as absence would unbar a second body for the Session.\n\nThe probe reads the tmux session first (it carries the `Present` veto, since `observe_provider` overwrites the group with the harness's child group for opencode and codex bodies, while the lf body always runs inside its recorded session), then **one** signal identity — the process group, falling back to the pid only when no group was recorded. Never both: a pid is a recycled resource, so on an old generation the recorded pid may name a stranger, and vetoing on it would pin the lease forever against any future evidence — the defect this exists to clear, rebuilt inside its own fix.\n\nThe release never re-signals. A stuck lease is old by construction, so retrying the kill would mean sending TERM to whatever inherited the pid; and a body proven gone has nothing left to kill.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `engine/process.rs`: `Presence` verdict, `probe_child_body_presence`, and pure classifiers for the errno table and tmux's stderr wordings — including `error connecting to … (No such file or directory)`, which is what tmux 3.6a actually prints when the socket is gone. Missing that wording would have made the feature a no-op on exactly the hosts it exists for: a host whose last body took the server with it reads `Unprovable` on every probe.\n- `ops/child.rs`: `release_dead_revoked_child_body`, plus a reap failure now attempting the release before surfacing the original error.\n- `ops/task.rs` / `ops/project.rs`: launch and strand-recovery release a dead lease before reserving, so recovery doesn't burn its attempt budget on a CAS that cannot pass.\n- A reservation lease-miss now names the lease instead of reporting \"changed from waiting to waiting; retry the command\" — a message that told a machine to retry something that can never pass.\n- `FakeTmux` test fixture pinning `PATH`, so a probe's verdict is a fact about the fixture rather than the host. Errno classification is proven over plain values: a real `EPERM` would be a fact about the CI user (as root none arises at all), and `Group(1)` is no fixture either — it negates to `kill(-1, 0)`, a broadcast over the machine.\n- Kernel-level test: a lease pinned by a dead group releases and only then reserves, with a live unrelated pid recorded as the generation's pid.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 975,
      "deletions": 15,
      "changed_files": 5
    },
    {
      "number": 1056,
      "title": "task: settle merged work over a proven-empty successor",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf task complete W2-304 \"merged work settled\"\ncargo test -p loopflow ops::task\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nWhen a Task's PR merges with `after_merge: Review`, the lifecycle rotates a serial successor. If the work is actually done, that successor is never published and never authored — it's an artifact of the rotation, not work. Today `lf task complete` refuses on it, and neither escape hatch is good: publishing opens a zero-commit PR, and abandoning leaves the Session `Waiting` so the next rotation mints PR N+1. This lets completion settle over that successor when — and only when — the branch can be *proven* to hold nothing.\n\nThe proof reuses the merged-follow-up classifier. `commits_past` is factored out of `committed_follow_up_range` so the same tri-state (`ProvenEmpty` / `Range` / `Unprovable`) and the same ancestry rule apply to a new cut: the PR's recorded `base_commit`. `Unprovable` matters because `is_ancestor` maps every nonzero exit to false — a rewritten branch and a missing object arrive identically, and neither proves emptiness. Only `ProvenEmpty`, and only behind a merged predecessor, is discardable.\n\nThe discard is part of completing, not a precondition. `task_completion_gate` classifies without mutating; `task_complete` passes the successor as the completion transaction's `skipped_pr`, so the row is dropped and the terminal status written together. There is no instant where the successor is gone and the Task is not yet terminal — the only state `ensure_working_pr_with_authority` would rotate a fresh empty PR from. The store enforces this too: skipping a PR is legal only while completing.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `commits_past` generalizes the follow-up classifier over an arbitrary cut; `unpublished_work` applies it to a PR's recorded base\n- `CompletionGate` carries `discardable_successor` — classification only, acted on by exactly one caller\n- `PrPhase::Working` blockers now say which of the three cases they hit: empty-with-no-merged-predecessor, committed follow-up, or unprovable\n- `advance_completion_after_gate` declines when a successor is discardable — that path settles through `complete_task_session_after_pr`, which has no `skipped_pr` and would leave a completed Task holding an active PR\n- Three tests prove the behavior: a merged Task settles over an empty successor (and reconcile doesn't walk it back or re-mint), a pending required review blocks while leaving the successor active, and a successor with committed work or an unprovable base is never discardable",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 384,
      "deletions": 23,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 1057,
      "title": "gate: record durable pre-land budget evidence",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/test.py            # run suites the branch touched\nuv run python scripts/test.py --all      # bounded full gate\nuv run python scripts/test.py --history 30  # judge the durable 30-day budget window\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThe pre-land gate already printed each phase's `elapsed / budget`, but that evidence died with the terminal — Task-worktree pruning and `.lf/tmp` reaping took it with them, so there was no way to answer \"has the gate stayed inside its budgets?\" over a real observation window. Every invocation now checkpoints a compact JSON record under the repository's Git common directory (`<git-common-dir>/loopflow/pre-land/runs/<kind>/<run-id>.json`), which linked worktrees share and which survives worktree removal, `.lf/tmp` cleanup, and restart. `--history 30` reads that window back and prints an explicit verdict.\n\nThe record holds only operational facts: run identity, branch, commit, timestamps, and per-phase status, elapsed time, and governing budget. Logs, commands, paths, output, diffs, and environment values stay out, and a test pins the schema shut.\n\nBecause `--all` records are the 30-day authority, losing one would bias the evidence — a full run whose record can't be written stops with `MEASUREMENT FAILED` before doing work it couldn't prove. Changed-aware and `--ui-host` runs are diagnostics: they print one `MEASUREMENT WARNING` and preserve their underlying test result.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `_run_command` returns a measured `PhaseOutcome` (budget, elapsed, status, over-budget) instead of an optional failure string; a run is checkpointed as `running` with every phase `not_run` before the first phase starts, so an interrupted gate reads as incomplete rather than absent.\n- Phases after a failure stay visible in the summary and the record as `not_run` with their budget, instead of vanishing.\n- `--history N` is read-only and reports `IN PROGRESS` (clean history younger than the window), `NOT HOLDING` (any over-budget, incomplete, or unreadable full record), or `HOLDING`. A corrupt `full` record is a gap; a corrupt `changed` record is not.\n- `GATE_BUDGET.md` documents the evidence path, the schema boundary, and the verdict rules; a test asserts the document names every executable phase budget, so a budget can't drift away from its published value.\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 924,
      "deletions": 46,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 1059,
      "title": "tasks: record a re-minted PR's base from the branch it forks from",
      "body": "",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 341,
      "deletions": 6,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 1060,
      "title": "task: scope required reviews to active epochs",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow ops::task::tests::superseded\ncargo test -p loopflow ops::task::tests::gate\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nA `ChangesRequested` review used to block Task completion forever. Once a Human requested changes at a Kickoff or Gate waitpoint, that review stayed attached to the Session and kept appearing in the completion gate's blocker list — even after the Session repaired the work, re-entered the gate, and got approved on the next cycle. The Task could never complete.\n\n`required_reviews_for_task` now only counts reviews that still speak for the current attempt: reviews at the Session's current `phase_epoch` (the live waitpoint), plus the newest Gate epoch, which carries the standing settlement verdict. Reviews from superseded epochs stay in the store as audit history without gating anything.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `required_reviews_for_task` filters on `phase_epoch` and `TaskLifecyclePhase::Gate`, keeping the current epoch and the newest Gate epoch\n- `open_gate_review` test helper returns its `ChildWriteLease` and accepts an existing one, so a test can open two reviews across gate cycles on one process generation\n- Three new proofs: a superseded Kickoff `ChangesRequested` no longer bars completion; the newest gate cycle's approval supersedes an earlier rejection; a live rejection from the current gate still blocks",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 153,
      "deletions": 32,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 1061,
      "title": "auth: demote strained accounts before provider refusal",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf route show\nlf usage\n```\n\n`lf route show` now marks accounts that health has demoted:\n\n```\ncodex  (loopflowstudio/loopflow)\n  1. engineering          engineering@example.com          connected  demoted: weekly 80% used\n  2. personal             personal@example.com             connected\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAutomatic account selection used to hand work to the first declared account until the provider refused it — the limit observations we already had sat unused until a run failed. Now an account with a provider-observed limit window at least 75% used and not yet reset falls behind unstrained accounts. Declared route order still decides everything else: the sort is stable, strained accounts stay eligible when they're the only choice, and session pins and explicit `--account` keep precedence.\n\nForwarded runs get the same treatment. Health lives in the local limit snapshot, so `local_forwarded_account_bundle` resolves the order on the host and forwards the resulting route rather than the limit rows behind it — a remote resolver with an empty store still tries the healthy account first.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `active_account_strain` picks the window that most justifies a demotion (highest utilization, ties broken on window name for stable evidence); only windows that are over the bar *and* still counting down demote — under the bar, already reset, or never resetting all leave declared order alone\n- `order_accounts_by_strain` sorts store-side automatic selection; `order_forwarded_routes_by_strain` bakes the same order into the forwarded bundle\n- `lf route show` renders the demotion where the order lives, with the window and utilization as evidence; `lf usage` still reports the windows behind it\n- Tests cover the four-way boundary (strained / under-bar / reset / no-reset), the only-choice case, and an end-to-end forwarded bundle asserting the healthy account's token reaches the spawned command\n- README documents the rule and the forwarding behavior",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 348,
      "deletions": 82,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 1062,
      "title": "ci-fix wake: refuse to arm on a land-time precondition",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\nlf ci W2-309        # head still reports red on scratch-clear\nlf task status W2-309   # no ci-fix command queued, no body woken\n```\n\n## Summary\n\n`scratch-clear` fails on every Task PR that carries its own design doc — that's every Task PR during kickoff and iterate, by construction — and the only thing that greens it is `lf pr land` clearing `scratch/`. No repair turn can act on it. Loopflow nonetheless minted a `CiIncident` and armed a `ci-fix` wake against that red, spending a full agent body on a bounded repair whose only route to green is deleting the design doc the reviewer is reading. Measured live three times: ENG-4 #1055, W2-297 #1060, and this PR's own head `904185190`.\n\nThis names that class — a *land-time precondition* — once, on `CiCheck`, and routes wake legality through it. A head whose failures are all land-time preconditions no longer arms a wake. The observation stays honest: `lf ci` and `lf task status` still name the failure, and the incident row still opens with a NULL trigger. Only the repair is refused.\n\nThe filter is deliberately narrow in both directions. A real leaf alongside `scratch-clear` still arms, and an *unnamed* failure still arms — a filter that swallows failures it cannot classify is a mute button, not a classifier. A name earns admission to the class only when an `lf pr land` step is what resolves it; \"hard to fix\" doesn't qualify.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `CiCheck::land_time_precondition()` plus the single `scratch-clear` literal, documented with the one rule for admitting a name.\n- `CiObservation::wake_legal()` gains the clause; consumers 1 (`current_ci_incident`) and 2 (`ci_fix_restart_bar`) both route through it, so both are fixed at the seam.\n- A test pins the literal against `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, so renaming the CI job turns the const red instead of silently re-arming wakes in production.\n- Lifecycle test through the real read path, using the check set taken verbatim from #1062: a scratch-clear-only head wakes nobody; a real leaf on the same head still earns exactly one attributable body.\n- `clear_scratch` in `ops/land.rs` gets a pointer back to the classifier, so the two ends of the contract find each other.\n\nThe design doc names all five consumers that ask \"is this failure actionable?\" and computes it as `state == Failing`. This PR fixes the two that arm work; the status/routing three (`open_pr_model`, `next_move`, `decide_open_pr_status`) are PR 2, and the last of them defuses in practice once no wake arms.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 202,
      "deletions": 2,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 1063,
      "title": "task: settle a bounded ci-fix turn above the parent lifecycle loop",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow ci_fix_lifecycle\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nA ci-fix body is one bounded repair turn, but its exit sat at the *tail* of the runner's parent-lifecycle loop — after rotation, the gate, and every other path that reads or rewrites a cursor the repair doesn't own. A repair's playhead is `ci-fix` while the phase's flow is `task-kickoff`, `task`, or `task-gate`, so those paths either rejected the playhead (Gate and Iterate, W2-280/W2-298/W2-303) or, worse, silently replaced it: Kickoff entered Iterate, discarded the `ci-fix` playhead for a fresh `task` flow, spent a `task_clarify` turn, and left the wake `Claimed` with its incident already stamped (W2-309, #1062). Nothing validated, so it survived #1054's cursor guard with a green suite.\n\nThis hoists the `settle_ci_fix_turn` call above the lifecycle loop, so the repair returns before any parent path can run. The exit fires when the flow iteration completed or the turn was interrupted; anything else is a repair still mid-flow. It also stops a repair from parking on the parent's interactive rendezvous — ci-fix boot skips the birth reconcile, so a prior body's handoff is still pending, and parking would write the `ci-fix` playhead into the cursor by a second route. The handoff stays pending for the next parent body, which is the one place allowed to claim its wake.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Repair exit moved above the parent-lifecycle loop; `settle_ci_fix_turn`'s doc now states where the call site must stand and why a new lifecycle path belongs below it.\n- The `parked_on_interactive_handoff` park is gated off when a ci-fix wake is live.\n- Tests drive the *real* runner (not a hand-called settlement — the defect is an ordering) via a shared `run_real_body` helper, and count provider sends: `sends == 1` is the load-bearing assertion, since every cursor check passes with the Kickoff hole fully present.\n- New cases: Iterate cursor preserved, Kickoff settles before Iterate and spends no lifecycle turn, and a repair settles past a pending handoff it does not own.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 300,
      "deletions": 45,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 1064,
      "title": "ci-fix: preempt a parked interactive review for an actionable wake",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow ci_fix_lifecycle\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nA ci-fix wake arrives while the Task body is alive and a provider turn owns the transcript. The runner correctly holds the repair until that turn releases — except a turn parked on an interactive review never releases. The agent is *waiting*, so no `TurnCompleted` ever comes, and the claimed wake sits `Claimed` with `responded_at` null until the body dies. This PR takes that boundary explicitly: when an interaction review is open and a claimed wake names the failure the PR reads as *now*, the runner interrupts once and lets the existing arm run the repair on the released boundary.\n\nThe preempt is deliberately narrow. It fires at most once per provider turn (cleared on `TurnCompleted`), and only for a *current, actionable* wake — the check routes through `current_ci_incident`, which already filters on `wake_legal`, so a head red only on `scratch-clear` yields no current incident and never reaches the interrupt. That direction is inherited, not restated: there is no `scratch-clear` literal in the preempt path. The interrupted review is left durable — clearing `interaction_review` is a runner local, so the next generation reopens the row and normal lifecycle review resumes.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Split the current-incident read out of `arm_ci_fix_wake` into `current_ci_incident_identity`, shared with the new read-only `holds_current_ci_fix_wake`, so the mint and the match cannot drift. The arm supersedes and stamps, so it can't answer the mid-turn question without committing to a repair.\n- One test harness now models both first-turn shapes via `release_first_turn_after`: `Some` is ordinary work that ends on its own, `None` is a parked review that only an interrupt releases. The interrupt emits `TurnCompleted` after a delay, so `interrupts == 1` means once-across-ticks rather than once-per-tick.\n- Corrected `a_live_generation_holds_ci_fix_until_its_provider_turn_is_idle`: it sat at Gate step 0 (`demo`, `interactive: true` under the standard plan's `Require` policy), so its \"review turn\" was a live human review and its `interrupts == 0` asserted the defect. Moved to Iterate step 0 (`task_clarify`, non-interactive); assertions unchanged, the name is now true.\n- Two new regressions prove both directions on that harness: an actionable wake preempts a parked review exactly once and settles the command it was born for; a scratch-clear-only wake leaves the review strictly alone.\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 379,
      "deletions": 37,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 1065,
      "title": "doctor: report the running lf's build revision against merged main",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf doctor\nlf doctor --json | jq '.store.build_source_revision, .checks[] | select(.name == \"binary-freshness\")'\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nA fix can merge to main without ever reaching the installed `lf`, and nothing on a supported surface says so — `lf --version` stayed at `0.11.3` across four merged commits the running binary was missing, and binary mtime says nothing about the tree it compiled from. The exact answer was already embedded: `build.rs` emits `LOOPFLOW_BUILD_SOURCE_REVISION` and `build_info::source_revision()` reads it, but no command reported or compared it.\n\n`lf doctor` now prints `store.build_source_revision` and always appends a `binary-freshness` check — including when the store fails to open, since a stale binary is a likely cause of that failure. The check locates the build checkout (falling back to the checkout containing the working directory), refreshes `origin/main` with an explicit single-ref refspec, confirms the embedded revision exists as an object before asking about ancestry, and classifies the result. When the binary is behind, it names every missing merged commit oldest-first with its subject. The check reports only; rebuilding stays an operator action.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `BuildFreshness` is four-way — `Current`, `Behind`, `OffMain`, `Unprovable` — so an absent object or a failed git call can never be mistaken for \"current\". A failed fetch is `Unprovable`, never a fallback to a possibly-stale local ref.\n- `git::is_ancestor` previously collapsed every non-zero exit into `false`, silently reporting \"not an ancestor\" when git actually errored. It now distinguishes exit 1 (real answer) from real failures.\n- New `git::commit_exists` and `git::commits_between` back the object check and the ordered missing-commit range.\n- Classifier tests build a throwaway `A -> B -> C` repo and stay offline. A supported-surface regression runs the real `lf doctor --json` binary with an empty `PATH`, so the refresh fails closed and the assertions cover the reported revision and the check's presence rather than live network state.\n- No revision was added to `BinaryProvenance`, `ChildProcessGeneration`, the store, or Task status — this answers the running-CLI question in `lf doctor`, it doesn't build a fleet inventory.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 364,
      "deletions": 4,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 1069,
      "title": "docs+website: retell loopflow agent-first, and capture the product live",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\nuv run python website/main.py            # serve the docs the way agents read them\nuv run python scripts/website_screens.py # capture the docs UI screens\nuv run python scripts/capture_screenshots.py --hero  # one live product shot\nuv run python scripts/check_website_screens.py       # reject stale/unproven captures (CI gate)\nlf commit                                # commit with a generated message\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThis branch rewrites the public story of loopflow around the agent API, the fleet, and \"no server,\" and rebuilds the site that serves it. The README shrinks from a sprawling reference into a landing page; the reference moves into `docs/` (new `agent-api.md`, `authoring.md`, `conducting.md`, reworked `index.md`, `lf.md`, `waves.md`, `architecture.md`, `config.md`, `getting-started.md`) with an agent-first writing pass. The operating contract now ships as a real skill (`skills/loopflow/SKILL.md`), and the docs are served the way agents actually read them. The homepage leads with the demo and the real building blocks, with contrast, code-chip, and steer-echo fixes. `website/main.py` is substantially simplified — the DB, dev, and Figma/Resend deploy plumbing come out, and deploy no longer stages runtime secrets by hand.\n\nScreenshotting splits into two honest pipelines instead of one manifest with optional everything: `website_screens.py` captures the docs UI, and `capture_screenshots.py` captures live product shots by hand, with `check_website_screens.py` gating CI so stale or unproven captures can't ship. Old committed `concerto-*`/`maestro-*`/logo binaries are removed in favor of freshly captured shots. `lf commit` gets a gate plus implementation, and the Mac app grows test-mode hooks used by the capture flow.\n\n## Changes\n\n- README becomes the landing page; the command reference and authoring guides live under `docs/`, published as a skill and pinned to the builtin contract by tests\n- Two capture pipelines (docs screens vs. live product shots) replace the monolithic `generate_screenshots.py`; a CI check rejects stale/unproven product captures\n- Website backend trimmed: `db.py`, `dev.py`, Figma/Resend secrets, and manual secret staging removed; deploy rides the current branch\n- `lf commit` gate and implementation; Mac app test-mode support for captures\n- New tests: website screens, capture rendering, README/index sync, agent-reader e2e, and skill/builtin alignment\n- Design and review notes under `scratch/` (living-website, docs redo, lf-docs review)",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 2969,
      "deletions": 3393,
      "changed_files": 66
    },
    {
      "number": 1070,
      "title": "ci-fix: settle against the authoritative repaired head",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf ci --json   # each incident now carries repaired_head_sha beside failed_head_sha\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nCloses the ci-fix settlement race (W2-320). When a ci-fix body pushes a repair, settlement was judging head advancement against a warm PR observation that still named the pre-turn head — so a fresh push could be misattributed or missed. The settlement path now reads GitHub live, bypassing both the store's observation cache and `gh`'s HTTP cache, and records the head the repair body actually shipped on the incident. Failure-to-response evidence now names both the head an incident failed on and the head that settled it.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `PrReadFreshness` (`Cached` / `Fresh`) on `observe_pr_by_number`; `Fresh` drops `gh api --cache` so the read reflects GitHub's live head, while control-command bursts keep coalescing on `Cached`.\n- `reconcile_task_pr_fresh_for_lease` threads `Fresh` through reconcile and skips the store observation cache; existing reconcile entry points stay `Cached`.\n- `repaired_head_sha` added to `CiIncident` / `CiIncidentDto` (surfaced on `lf ci --json`, `null` until repaired) and persisted via a first-write-only `mark_ci_incident_repaired` store method.\n- Migration `0.11.029_ci_incident_repaired_head` adds the column.\n- Lifecycle tests cover the fresh-read settlement and the JSON surface; design notes under `scratch/`.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 414,
      "deletions": 81,
      "changed_files": 12
    },
    {
      "number": 1071,
      "title": "lf ssh: forward account authority through a fixed lease broker",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# Prefer accounts for one run; children inherit the same grant\nlf --account claude=personal --account codex=reserve implement\n\n# Restrict the process tree to exactly the selected accounts\nlf --only-account codex=reserve review\n\n# Forward the selected route to a remote host over a foreground broker\nlf --account claude=personal ssh mini -- lf task pursue\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nReplaces the `--account`/`LF_ACCOUNT` env-passing model with a lease broker that resolves managed Claude and Codex accounts once at the outer invocation and hands the process tree a fixed, opaque grant. `--account` prefers each matching account before its provider's normal route; `--only-account` removes every unselected account so a provider without a match is simply unavailable. The two flags are repeatable, accept `claude=`/`codex=` forms, and cannot be combined. Nested `lf` processes inherit the grant through the handle and cannot widen, narrow, or reorder it.\n\nOver SSH, accounts resolve on the origin machine and the remote receives only a short-lived lease handle; a reverse-forwarded owner-only Unix socket reaches a broker owned by the foreground `lf ssh` process. No managed credential files land on the remote host. Broker failure, missing credentials, and expired handles fail closed; detached forms (`tmux`, `nohup`, `--detach`, second SSH hops) are rejected. Under a forwarded lease, remote account/route edits and subscription polling are disabled, while `lf usage` still reports local process token spend.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `provider_account/lease.rs` holds the broker, `AccountSelection`, and forwarded-authority probe; `provider_account.rs` shrinks accordingly\n- Local commands with a selection get an in-process broker; SSH and remote-Home routing build and forward theirs in the transport path\n- `home::route` threads the account selection so remote-Home commands carry the grant\n- New `docs/security.md` covering execution boundaries, action policy, identity/lease forwarding, and stored/transmitted data; linked from the README\n- `docs/config.md` and `docs/lf.md` updated for managed accounts, access profiles, routes, and the `--account`/`--only-account` semantics\n- Website e2e/docs and accessibility tests updated for the new security page",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 2653,
      "deletions": 1738,
      "changed_files": 23
    },
    {
      "number": 1072,
      "title": "pm: bind infrastructure/intelligence/product to their own Linear teams",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf pm reteam --wave infrastructure   # dry run\nlf pm reteam --wave intelligence\nlf pm reteam --wave product\n```\n\nEach dry run now resolves the wave-owned team and defers live Task bodies instead of renumbering them:\n\n```\nwave/infrastructure → team 08c8d501-791d-4db0-a14c-15599d365955 (ENG-*)  [dry run]\n  deferred — protected writing Task body (3): W2-320, W2-321, W2-319\n  left as historical: 64 completed issue(s) stay in the shared team\nwave/intelligence  → team 7de894bd-17da-403a-9d71-93c40afe9367 (SCI-*)  [dry run]\nwave/product       → team e894ffa1-bc38-4382-af89-2e1d89884f4e (PRD-*)  [dry run]\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nPer-wave team support and reteam mechanics already shipped, but the three GOAL.md team bindings never landed on main — all three waves still pointed at the shared W2 team `60558c53`, so new Projects and Tasks kept receiving W2 identifiers and `lf pm reteam` aimed the wrong way. This PR flips the `pm.linear_team` value in each wave's GOAL.md frontmatter so infrastructure, intelligence, and product each own their identifier namespace (ENG-*, SCI-*, PRD-*), retiring the wave-team vs issue-team split that blocked closing an ENG-* issue under a W2-bound wave.\n\nThe change is binding only. Running `lf pm reteam --apply` (which renumbers issues) is a separate, flow-gated phase that happens after the in-flight infrastructure bodies (W2-319/320/321) settle, so no live body writes an identifier being renumbered. This branch is inert and mergeable while those bodies run.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `wave/{infrastructure,intelligence,product}/GOAL.md`: swap the shared W2 `linear_team` UUID for the wave-owned team; initiative ids and all other frontmatter untouched.\n- `scratch/complete-the-per-wave-linear.md`: design note capturing the problem, de-risking, and verified dry-run output (removed on `lf pr land`).\n- Verification is the live dry-run against the edited files, not a new unit test — a data-only value swap needs no code test and the dry-run exercises the full parse→resolve path.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 3,
      "deletions": 3,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 1073,
      "title": "architecture: establish the durable Run and Steer control spine",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf work --help\nlf launch --help\n\ncargo nextest run -p loopflow only_the_active_parent_run_can_steer_child_work\ncargo nextest run -p loopflow seed_only_wave_services_child_once_without_advancing_background\ncargo nextest run -p loopflow live_wave_preempts_background_for_child_and_preserves_playhead\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nReplace overlapping interaction and execution truths with a durable control\nspine: stable Work/Epoch identity, Basis-fenced Steers, exact Run authority,\nprovider/process Launches, optional observed Turns, typed Waits, and derived\nReview attention. Codex may live-send while Claude, OpenCode, and opaque TUIs\nseed another boundary; durable outcome and fencing stay the same.\n\nTask and Project still execute through the existing Session controller in this\nlanding. Every such body now registers its real process as a Launch under the\nmirrored Run, so Run interrupt and steering never fall back to Session lookup.\nA follow-up Task owns the one-way runner rewrite and Session deletion.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Delete stored `InteractionReview`, `InteractiveHandoff`, and `ChildCommand`\n  aggregates and their commands, DTOs, stores, and Swift surfaces.\n- Make Steer/Send/Basis authoritative with one opaque Run capability that\n  fails closed when missing, stale, or stopped.\n- Keep Review route open while pending attention parks; re-arm it once on the\n  child's next terminal Turn and advance parent evidence once.\n- Service direct input and oldest child Review before Wave/Project background\n  work, preserving the background playhead for live and seed-only providers.\n- Store root assistant output and normalized usage on Turn; derive monitoring\n  and spend from Run → Launch → Turn.\n- Register new, recovered, and migrated legacy bodies as product Launches and\n  close those Launches when their process boundary ends.\n- Remove retired controls from user docs, built-in skills, smoke tests, and\n  fixtures.\n\n## Rebase decisions\n\nCurrent main's `task reconcile` repairs orphaned ChildCommands and an\nunincorporated directive completion gate. This branch removes both underlying\nconcepts: no ChildCommand can orphan, and Task completion reads Review/Basis\nrather than directive acknowledgement. Keeping the command would recreate a\ndeleted compatibility model, so the architecture supersedes it while retaining\nmain's Linear attachment, migration release boundary, promotion fence, and PM\nfixes.\n\n## Follow-up\n\nRewrite Task and Project execution through shared Run `reserve | advance |\nstop`, then delete `ProjectSessionStatus`, `TaskSessionStatus`,\n`ChildWriteLease`, body generations, legacy authority env vars, duplicate\nrunners, and Session lifecycle/process schema. That Task also owns keeper\nrecovery, the final schema draft, zero Session-controller references, and the\n121,819-line ceiling.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 14887,
      "deletions": 27332,
      "changed_files": 162
    },
    {
      "number": 1074,
      "title": "install: read-only global-promotion preflight (W2-319 PR1)",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf install preflight          # human-readable promotion verdict\nlf install preflight --json   # structured PromotionPreview\n```\n\nExits non-zero when promotion is refused, so a caller can gate on it.\n\n## Summary\n\nA branch-local build must never silently become the machine-global `lf`. On 2026-07-17 a `--use` promotion repointed `~/.local/bin/lf` at a binary whose migration registry ended at `0.11.026` while the shared store was at `0.11.027`, and every subsequent invocation — including live bodies mid-turn — hit a store its own binary could not read. This PR is the first serial slice: the **read-only preflight**. The candidate binary (the process running the command) reads the shared store's applied migration frontier and its own registry, counts live Task/Project bodies, and renders a promotion verdict. It only reads — the mutating `promote`/`rollback` publication lands in the next PR and consumes this decision as its source of truth.\n\nCompatibility is not re-derived: `classify_compatibility` calls the exact `store::migrations` functions the runtime trusts at open time, so a reject reason is the store's own error string rather than a second registry. Everything fails closed — a missing, unopenable, or unreadable store, or an unreadable live-body set, all resolve to `Unreadable` and refuse rather than assume an empty/safe state.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `lf install preflight` command in `commands/install.rs`, dispatched in `bin/lf.rs` *before* home routing, journal emission, and any ordinary store open — a frontier-incompatible candidate reaches the refusal instead of dying in trace/store capture. Opens the store read-only inside its own preflight.\n- `PromotionPreview` decision types: `CandidateIdentity`, `Compatibility` (`Exact`/`AheadPending`/`Incompatible`/`Unreadable`), `LiveBody`, and `Verdict` (`Promote`/`PromoteAndMigrate`/`Reject`). A `Reject` carries every failing reason at once so one run names all blockers.\n- `decide()` is pure over its inputs (authority + compatibility + live bodies) with full unit-test coverage of each branch: exact frontier promotes, a validation-only build ahead of the store is rejected, a published build ahead promotes-and-migrates, incompatible/unreadable evidence fails closed, and any live body (lease `reserved`/`active`) blocks replacement.\n- `MigrationAuthority` now derives `Serialize` (snake_case) so it renders in the JSON preview.\n- Design doc and open questions under `scratch/` for the W2-319 serial project.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 522,
      "deletions": 2,
      "changed_files": 5
    },
    {
      "number": 1076,
      "title": "migrations: author ordinal-free drafts, canonicalize at the release cut (ENG-23)",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# Author a draft — no ordinal, no git fetch, no rebase contention\nuv run python scripts/new_migration.py add_wave_colour\nuv run python scripts/new_migration.py backfill_colour --depends-on add_wave_colour\n\n# CI / release: prove drafts are well-formed and orderable\nuv run python scripts/check_migrations.py\n\n# At the release cut: freeze drafts into canonical, ordered, ordinal-assigned migrations\nuv run python scripts/canonicalize_migrations.py 0.11.30\nuv run python scripts/canonicalize_migrations.py 0.11.30 --check   # plan only, writes nothing\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nMigration ordinals used to be allocated at authoring time, so two branches racing to add a migration would contend for the same serial number — a sibling could merge your ordinal while your PR sat, and a branch merely behind main's migrations would collide on merge (ENG-23). This makes authoring merge-independent: `new_migration.py` now writes a **draft** at `migrations/drafts/<name>.sql` carrying a stable snake_case name and **no ordinal**, and performs no `git fetch` or rebase. Ordering that matters is declared explicitly with `--depends-on`, not implied by a serial number.\n\nThe release PR becomes the single publication boundary. `canonicalize_migrations.py` freezes the draft set, rejects missing or cyclic dependencies, topologically orders it (edges first, ties broken by name — never merge time, PR number, or wall clock), assigns the next contiguous ordinals in the version being cut (a patch continues the current `<major>.<minor>`; a minor bump restarts at ordinal 1), writes `<major>.<minor>.<ordinal>_<name>.sql`, appends the `Migration` entry, and deletes the draft. It is deterministic, so an aborted release regenerates identical ids.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `new_migration.py` authors ordinal-free drafts into `migrations/drafts/`, validates `--depends-on` targets and name collisions, and never touches git\n- New `canonicalize_migrations.py` performs the release cut: topological order, contiguous ordinal assignment, registry append, draft deletion; supports `--check`\n- `check_migrations.py` validates drafts (well-formed name/header, resolvable acyclic `depends_on`, no released-name collision) and no longer compares drafts against `origin/main`, so a behind-main branch stays green\n- The Rust registration check ignores non-file entries so the `drafts/` directory and its README don't break it\n- `MIGRATIONS.md` rewritten around the draft → release-cut model; new `drafts/README.md`",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 794,
      "deletions": 105,
      "changed_files": 9
    },
    {
      "number": 1077,
      "title": "lf install: promote — CLI half of global promotion (W2-319 PR2a)",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# Preview only — validate and print the verdict, change nothing\nlf install promote --cli-target ~/.local/bin/lf --preview\n\n# Promote this build to the global CLI\nlf install promote --cli-target ~/.local/bin/lf\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThis lands the mutating half of global `lf` promotion. The read-only preflight (PR1) already decides whether the candidate binary is compatible with the shared store; `lf install promote` now consumes that verdict and performs the actual CLI swap. The candidate (the running binary) content-addresses itself into `~/.lf/bin`, retains immutable rollback bytes for the prior binary, and atomically repoints the target symlink — all under a new machine-global exclusive promotion lock whose shared side fences every Task and Project body reservation. Activation happens *before* any migration advances the store frontier, so the machine-global command is always compatible with whatever frontier is on disk. A `Reject` verdict leaves every target untouched.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `lf install promote --cli-target <path> [--preview]`: new subcommand that promotes the running build to the global CLI under the exclusive promotion lock.\n- `promotion_lock`: new machine-global fence; reservations hold the shared side across their lease CAS, promotion holds the exclusive side from under-lock recount through activation and migration.\n- Content-addressed binary store in `~/.lf/bin`: binaries staged read-only (`0o555`), fsynced, digested, and published by atomic rename; an existing digest is reused after a byte check and a mismatch is refused rather than overwriting a retained artifact.\n- Prior binary preserved into immutable storage (resolving symlink targets) so rollback bytes survive a mutable or replaced worktree target.\n- CLI symlink repointed via atomic temp-symlink + rename so the global target is never absent mid-swap.\n- Migration application follows activation for `PromoteAndMigrate`, so a frontier failure always leaves the compatible candidate global; tests cover staging, atomic commit, rollback retention, refusal, and the frontier-failure path.\n- App-bundle swap and `install.py` cutover are deferred to later serial PRs (see `scratch/questions.md`).",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 567,
      "deletions": 7,
      "changed_files": 7
    },
    {
      "number": 1078,
      "title": "pm reteam: move legacy issues before narrowing projects",
      "body": "## Summary\n\n- move every foreign-team issue, including completed issues, before narrowing its Linear Project\n- fail closed on live writing bodies and Projects missing the target team\n- preserve prior identifiers before the irreversible move and reconcile Task Sessions by stable issue UUID\n\n## Verification\n\n- rust-lint and rust-test passed on the reviewed head\n- all other actionable CI leaves passed\n- scratch-clear and tests-result remain structural land-time failures resolved by this command",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 859,
      "deletions": 120,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 1079,
      "title": "task: make caller authority an explicit invocation input",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf task steer ENG-123 \"tighten the retry window\"\nlf task resume ENG-123 --reason \"picking up after handoff\"\nlf project start \"Wave Chat parity\" --wave w2\n```\n\nSame commands, no new flags — the change is under the hood. Every Task and Project control command now resolves *who is calling* (Operator, Wave, or Project) once at the CLI boundary and threads that authority down explicitly, instead of re-deriving provenance from ambient environment deeper in the stack.\n\n## Summary\n\nCaller authority (W2-281) was previously inferred at multiple points in the control path, which let the Task and Project routes drift and could misattribute a command's source. This PR introduces a single `CallerAuthority` type (`Operator` / `Wave(WaveId)` / `Project(ProjectSessionId)`) resolved at the invocation boundary and passed through `task_*` and `project_*` ops. The resolution rule — fail-closed on an inconsistent managed session, live-route validation for a Project caller — now has one home and maps 1:1 onto the stored `ChildCommandSource`. `project start` preserves wave-authority precedence by resolving an explicit `--wave` argument ahead of the ambient wave.\n\nAlongside the authority plumbing, Move 2 adds a truthful fast-bar for supervisor `Resume`: a resume of a non-active body that is already barred (open PR, publishing, or terminal) refuses *before* any command row is written, so the common steady-Open case leaves nothing in the ledger. When a `Working → Open` PR flip races launch after the row exists, the still-`Persisted` command is terminalized to `Failed` via a new pre-delivery `reject_persisted_child_command` primitive, so no orphaned command lingers.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `CallerAuthority` enum in `child_session` with `into_source()`; `resolve_caller_authority` at the ops boundary\n- All `task_*`/`project_*` control ops and their CLI dispatch take an explicit authority argument\n- `project start` resolves an explicit `--wave` before falling back to ambient wave authority\n- Command events now carry an optional error string; `reject_persisted_child_command` store primitive terminalizes pre-delivery rejections\n- Supervisor `Resume` fast-bar refuses barred, non-active bodies before writing a command row; race after creation terminalizes the persisted command to `Failed`\n- New `caller_authority_cli_tests` plus live-route and steady-Open fast-bar regressions; a `#[cfg(test)]` before-launch hook makes the post-persist/pre-launch PR race deterministic",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1161,
      "deletions": 212,
      "changed_files": 17
    },
    {
      "number": 1081,
      "title": "migrations: make the release cut the executable canonicalization boundary (ENG-24)",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# Author a draft — file-only, no ordinal, no registry edit, no git\npython3 scripts/new_migration.py add_wave_colour\npython3 scripts/new_migration.py backfill_colour --depends-on add_wave_colour\n\n# The release cut freezes drafts into canonical migrations\nlf release 0.11.30\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThis moves migration canonicalization to a single, executable boundary: the release cut. Authoring a draft is now fully merge-independent — `new_migration.py` writes one file named `<name>__<128-bit token>.sql`, allocates no ordinal, and touches neither the Rust registry nor git. Two branches authoring the same readable name mint distinct 128-bit tokens and produce a byte-identical `migrations.rs`, so there is nothing to contend on and no merge conflict. The release run (`prepare_release_in_worktree`) invokes `canonicalize_migrations.py` after the version bump and before the commit, so the generated `migrations.rs` and `.sql` files land in the release PR and run under real Rust CI before the queue merges and tags. Canonicalization is atomic: a write failure restores the tree byte-for-byte — registry, canonical dir, and drafts all unchanged.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Release is the publication authority**: `ops/release.rs` gains `canonicalize_migrations`, wired into the worktree release sequence. Targets with no canonicalization script skip it; the script is stdlib-only so the release needs only a `python3` interpreter.\n- **128-bit draft identity**: draft filenames carry a 32-hex-char token (up from a collision-prone 32-bit token), with an `-- id:` header that must agree with the filename. `__` is reserved as the name/id separator.\n- **Cross-boundary dependencies**: `--depends-on` accepts both drafts and already-released migration names; depending on a released migration imposes no in-cut ordering. Unknown names fail with a clear message.\n- **Sabotage-sensitive coverage**: a Rust test drives the real production release sequence (not the helper directly), so deleting the canonicalization call site fails the build. A durable `store_0_10_release.sql` fixture anchors the prior-release regression.\n- **Tests + docs**: updated canonicalize/check/new-migration Python suites for the token-aware draft shape, atomic-rollback proof, and same-name concurrency; refreshed `MIGRATIONS.md` and `drafts/README.md`.\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1018,
      "deletions": 130,
      "changed_files": 11
    },
    {
      "number": 1082,
      "title": "lf install: route every global mutation through the rust promotion boundary (ENG-25)",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# Refresh the installed CLI from a worktree build (staged by Python, committed by Rust)\nlf install refresh\n\n# Promote a fresh local build — CLI, app bundle, bundled helper, and skills\nlf install local --use\n\n# Roll the global symlink back to a retained, store-compatible binary\nlf install promote --cli-target ~/.local/bin/lf --app-source ... --sync-skills\nlf install rollback --cli-target ~/.local/bin/lf --candidate ~/.lf/bin/lf-<digest>\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nFinishes the global-install cutover so that `install.py` only ever stages branch-local build artifacts, and every machine-global mutation runs through the Rust `install promote` boundary under the exclusive promotion lock. Python no longer copies binaries, swaps the app, or runs skill sync directly — it hands the staged CLI, app bundle, legacy target, and a `--sync-skills` flag to `lf install promote`, which owns CLI activation, app replacement, migration advancement, rollback validation, and post-commit skill sync. This kills the split-brain where two languages could each half-perform a global install.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `install promote` gains `--app-source`, `--app-target`, `--legacy-app-target`, and `--sync-skills`; a new `install rollback` repoints the global symlink to a retained binary only after validating it's still store-compatible (rejects binaries that would advance migrations or fail preflight).\n- App-bundle promotion stages a fsynced copy, then commits by rename with the old bundle moved to a unique sidecar — a mid-commit rename failure restores the old app, and a crash never leaves a partially copied bundle. The fallible app copy is staged *before* the safety-critical CLI commit, so an unreadable bundle leaves every global target untouched.\n- `scripts/install.py` `refresh()` and `local()` now route the CLI symlink, app swap, bundled helper, and skills through the promotion boundary instead of doing direct copies; `_promote` and the standalone `_sync_skills` path are gone.\n- Tests route the fake `lf` through the real promotion boundary (both the install-script and release-automation fakes), parametrize refresh over `no_pull`, and prove `commit_app_bundle` restores the old app on a mid-commit rename failure.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 789,
      "deletions": 104,
      "changed_files": 6
    },
    {
      "number": 1083,
      "title": "lf install: validate the bundled app helper is the promoted candidate",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf install promote --cli-target <path> --app-source <staged.app> --app-target <installed.app>\nlf install rollback --cli-target <path> --candidate <retained-lf>\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nApp promotion now proves the `lf` helper embedded in the staged `.app` bundle is the same candidate that passed preflight before it commits the bundle. Previously the CLI symlink and the app bundle were promoted from independent sources, so a stale or mismatched bundled helper could land alongside a freshly promoted CLI. Staging an app bundle now runs the bundled `Contents/MacOS/lf` through `install preflight --json` and refuses to continue unless its candidate identity and verdict match the promotion preview — leaving both the CLI and the existing app untouched on mismatch.\n\nTo do this, the preflight reader was widened from verdict-only to the full candidate+verdict payload, which also lets rollback confirm a retained binary's identity, not just its compatibility. `CandidateIdentity` and `MigrationAuthority` gain `Deserialize` so the preview can round-trip through the helper's JSON.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `stage_app_bundle` validates the bundled helper against the expected candidate and verdict before staging succeeds; failure cleans up and aborts before the CLI is touched\n- `read_rollback_verdict` → `read_binary_preflight`, returning `{candidate, verdict}`; rollback extracted into `rollback_from_store`\n- `render_retained_prior` now emits a complete `rollback` command including `--cli-target`\n- New tests cover happy-path app commit ordering, a mismatched bundled helper leaving CLI/app untouched, and an incompatible retained binary never activating as a rollback",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 225,
      "deletions": 125,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 1084,
      "title": "task: recommend and route only CI failures a body can repair (ENG-33)",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\nlf task status W2-309   # head red only on scratch-clear\n# before: action: resume, blocked: review, owner=Ci\n# after:  action: review, owner=Review\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nA design-carrying Task PR is red on `scratch-clear` by construction pre-land — that check only greens when `lf pr land` clears `scratch/`. Consumers that ask \"is this failing head actionable by a body?\" were computing it as `ci.state == Failing`, conflating \"red\" with \"a body can fix this\". #1062 fixed the two wake-arming askers; this closes the two supervision-surface consumers so a doomed Resume is never recommended and the reviewer's turn is never labelled \"fixing CI\".\n\nThe fix adopts the predicate already established on `main` rather than re-deriving it: a new `CiObservation::only_land_time_preconditions()` — the dual of `wake_legal()` within the failing state (Failing, at least one named failure, every failure a land-time precondition). An unclassified or real-leaf failure keeps the head owned by CI, preserving the anti-mute-button rule.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `task/mod.rs`: add `CiObservation::only_land_time_preconditions()` plus a test pinning it as the dual of `wake_legal`.\n- `task/actions.rs`: `open_pr_model` routes a precondition-only head to the reviewable model (recommend Review, block Resume), factored into a shared `open_pr_reviewable(reason)`.\n- `lf/commands/waves.rs`: `next_move_for_task` returns `owner=Review` for such a head before the `fixing` check, so a live body on it is the reviewer's turn.\n- Sabotage-sensitive tests on both consumers: reverting each change reddens its first test; the real-leaf test passes with the bug fully present.\n- Product call made in the open: a design-carrying PR is reviewable while `scratch-clear` is red.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 202,
      "deletions": 16,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 1085,
      "title": "pm: resolve Task completion state from the issue's owning team",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf pm task done --id <ENG issue> --pr https://github.com/.../pull/1035\n```\n\nAn `ENG-*` close now succeeds where it used to fail with *\"Discrepancy between issue team and state, cycle or project.\"*\n\n## Summary\n\nA wave pins one Linear team, but a Project can span teams (Developer Efficiency carries both `ENG-*` and `W2-*`). `complete_item`/`reopen_item` resolved the target workflow state from the wave-configured team and then handed Linear a state id belonging to a team the issue was not in — which Linear rejects. This PR resolves the workflow state from the issue's *own* team instead. It also fixes an ordering bug in `lf pm task done`: the PR link was commented *before* the close, so a rejected close left a durable \"Shipped:\" comment on a still-open issue. State now transitions before the comment, so the comment follows the state.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `ISSUE_TEAM_QUERY` + `item_team_id()` helper reads an existing issue's owning team; `completed_state_id` takes a `team_id` param (mirroring `unstarted_state_id`) instead of resolving the wave team internally.\n- `complete_item` and `reopen_item` resolve state from the issue's team; `create_item`/`create_project` still use the wave-configured team, so new issues land in the wave's team.\n- `apply_update` transitions the issue to Done before posting the PR-link comment.\n- Regression tests: a client whose configured team differs from the issue's team completes/reopens by reading the issue team (sabotaging to the wave team goes red); the comment is asserted to follow the state transition.\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 156,
      "deletions": 19,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 1086,
      "title": "store: make shared DB migrations exclusive to the promotion boundary",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf install promote\nlf install preflight --json\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAdvancing the shared release store `~/.lf/loopflow.db` past the migration frontier the installed `lf` knows is no longer a side effect of an ordinary command. On 2026-07-17 a published candidate at `target/release/lf` migrated the shared store during a normal open and stranded the installed binary. This PR makes the shared frontier exclusive to the promotion boundary: only `lf install promote` — under its exclusive promotion lock and drained live-body fence — opens the store as `Authorized` and applies the pending migration. Ordinary opens read and validate the shared store but never advance it, and validation-only builds never write to it at all. Private/isolated dev stores are unaffected — the caller still initializes and advances them freely.\n\nBootstrapping a missing or empty shared store is folded into the same rule: initializing to the candidate's head can strand an older installed binary exactly as advancing an existing frontier would, so both belong to the boundary alone. Under the exclusive promotion lock an *absent* store is positive proof of zero persisted live leases and a promotable uninitialized frontier, so a published candidate reaches `PromoteAndMigrate` and first initialization happens through the authorized open. An existing-but-empty or corrupt file is not the fresh-init case and keeps failing closed.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `FrontierAdvance` (`Forbidden` / `Authorized`) threaded through `may_apply_migrations`; the shared store now requires both `Published` authority and the authorized boundary to migrate.\n- `SqliteStore::new` opens `Forbidden` (read/validate only); new `open_as_promotion_boundary` opens `Authorized`, called from `lf install promote`.\n- `read_store_evidence` splits liveness/compatibility reading out of `build_preview`; an absent store now previews as an `AheadPending` uninitialized frontier rather than `Unreadable`.\n- `pending_shared_migration` names the exact head an ordinary shared open refuses on; regression tests cover the shorter-frontier / old-reader-still-recognizes case and the promotion boundary being the only writer that advances the frontier.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 473,
      "deletions": 60,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 1087,
      "title": "lf task reconcile: settle a merged, Linear-complete Task via Wave/Operator attestation",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf task reconcile ENG-42 \\\n  --directive 3 \\\n  --summary \"final direction shipped in the merged head; attested by operator\"\n\n# machine-readable\nlf task reconcile ENG-42 --directive 3 --summary \"...\" --json\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAdds an out-of-band path to settle a Task that is stuck in a specific, un-recoverable shape: its work merged and Linear reports it complete, but its *final* directive was applied to a body that never acknowledged it. That Task can't rotate a successor PR (the work already shipped in the merged head) and can't be recovered (there's nothing left to relaunch), so it strands under the supervisor forever. `lf task reconcile` lets a Wave or Operator explicitly attest that the applied directive was in fact incorporated, records that attestation as the incorporation evidence, drives the Session through the normal completion gate to `Completed`, and clears the orphaned recovery command in place — with no provider turn and no new PR.\n\nReconciliation is *permitted* only for the exact reconcilable shape (current directive `applied_at` set but unincorporated, newest PR a merged `CompleteTask`, no active body); the incorporation *evidence* is never synthesized — the caller must name the directive version and supply a non-empty semantic summary. The supervisor is taught to recognize this shape and leave the `Reconcile` recommendation standing rather than relaunching a body, rotating a PR, or queuing a CI fix.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `TaskCommand::Reconcile` (`--directive`, `--summary`, `--json`) wired through `lf.rs` and `ops::task::task_reconcile`, returning the terminal Session, who attested, and any cleared orphaned commands.\n- Extracted `settle_completed_session` from `task_complete` so both in-body/operator completion and out-of-band reconciliation share the clean-tree check, completion gate, PM writeback, terminal transaction, and events.\n- New `latest_pr_completes_task` — the single \"this Task settled on a completing merge\" predicate keyed on the *newest* PR by sequence, shared by the reconcile guard, supervisor suppression, and `merged_completing_pr` (so an older `CompleteTask` behind a later `Review` merge never drives completion).\n- New `current_directive` / `current_directive_applied` helpers; the applied-but-unincorporated directive no longer authorizes a successor PR, and `snapshot`/wave detail now surface `directive_applied`.\n- `reject_orphaned_persisted_commands` terminalizes stranded `Persisted` recovery commands in place; new `DirectiveReconciled` activity rendering; kickoff + design doc under `scratch/`.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1390,
      "deletions": 137,
      "changed_files": 8
    },
    {
      "number": 1089,
      "title": "pm: fix Linear PR-attachment argument and make a wrong mutation argument fail CI",
      "body": "attachmentLinkURL rejects subtitle (400 on every published PR since #1010). Remove it from the create mutation/variable/call chain; attachmentUpdate keeps subtitle (its input accepts it) and the managed comment body already embeds PR state. The bug shipped green because the test mocked success; now the invariants are pinned directly — the create mutation excludes subtitle, the update mutation retains it, and the serialized create request carries no subtitle variable. Closes ENG-79.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 45,
      "deletions": 22,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 1090,
      "title": "lf task reconcile: trust the settled PR set plus actual Linear completion (ENG-113)",
      "body": "## Summary\n\nThe live ENG-29 shape — an older merged `CompleteTask`, a later merged `Review`, and a trailing abandoned unpublished successor, under an applied-but-unincorporated final directive with a Linear-complete issue — could not be reconciled. All three reconcile consumers keyed on `latest_pr_completes_task` (the newest PR must be a merged `CompleteTask`), so the abandoned successor and the later `Review` made `lf task reconcile` refuse, the supervisor relaunch a body, and the action model recommend `StartNextPr`. The Task stranded forever.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `reconcilable_pr_set` predicate: ignore abandoned unpublished successor rows (select the latest non-abandoned PR), require no active or unmerged non-abandoned PR, and accept the latest non-abandoned PR in any merged disposition (`Review` or `CompleteTask`).\n- Automatic completion stays strict — `latest_pr_completes_task` is unchanged and remains the sole input to `merged_completing_pr`.\n- Rewire the reconcile command guard, supervisor Reconcile suppression, and the action-model recommendation onto `reconcilable_pr_set`. The action model now derives Reconcile from a whole-set `reconcilable` signal in one place, subsuming the old `CompleteTask`-only block.\n\n## Tests\n\n- Exact ENG-29 regression: merged `CompleteTask`, later merged `Review`, abandoned unpublished successor, applied unincorporated directive, Linear complete → reconcile succeeds, Session `Completed`, orphaned recovery command cleared; snapshot recommends `reconcile`.\n- Preserved sabotage: a later non-abandoned open/unmerged `Review` refuses.\n- Unit coverage for `reconcilable_pr_set`; automatic-completion strictness tests unchanged.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 421,
      "deletions": 85,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 1091,
      "title": "testing: focus local verification on the smallest useful proof",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/test.py --list            # affected-suite plan\nuv run python scripts/test.py --reuse-passing    # affected suites once per exact tree\nuv run python scripts/test_time.py --days 7      # where agent verification time went\nlf testing-audit                                 # audit test value and workflow\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThis branch reorients verification around running the smallest proof that can change the next decision, instead of rerunning broad suites at every lifecycle phase. It adds a \"Verification Cadence\" doctrine to STYLE.md and the build skills so each phase (implement, compress, lint, rebase, gate, CI/release, demo) owns exactly one proof level, and it teaches the changed-aware runner to reuse a prior pass only when tracked/untracked content, the worktree, and the command plan are all identical.\n\nThe gate's durable budget-history machinery (`GATE_BUDGET.md`, `--history`, HOLDING/NOT HOLDING verdicts) is removed in favor of exact-tree and plan fingerprints plus a trace-ledger time report (`scripts/test_time.py`), which merges parallel intervals per launch and prints only aggregate categories and skills — never commands, prompts, or output. A new `testing-audit` build skill finds low-value tests and redundant verification and improves the workflow.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `testing-audit` builtin skill, surfaced in README, `docs/lf.md`, and the build lifecycle skills\n- Verification Cadence section in STYLE.md; TESTING.md rewritten around focused proofs and the nine-job `tests-result` aggregate\n- `scripts/test.py` gains `--reuse-passing` (exact-tree + plan reuse); drops the budget-history window and `--history`\n- New `scripts/test_time.py` trace-timing toolkit with `test_test_time.py` coverage\n- `test_gate_bounded.py` retargeted from history verdicts to evidence-reuse and measurement-warning behavior\n- Nightly package smoke now exercises `lf --help` and `lf --list`\n- Refreshed demo/implement builtin goldens",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1170,
      "deletions": 702,
      "changed_files": 23
    },
    {
      "number": 1092,
      "title": "release: v0.12.0",
      "body": "# v0.12.0\n\nv0.12.0 answers four questions that v0.11.3 left ambient: *who* runs the work, *as whom* it spends, *on which machine* it lives, and *how it recovers when no one is watching*. Provider Accounts become the thing routes select and bill against; a Wave gains an addressable Home you can probe, start, and forward authority to over SSH; a Task now repairs its own red CI and recovers its own stranded bodies without a human; and every Wave owns its own Linear team instead of sharing one namespace. Underneath, one hard rule now governs upgrades: only an official release install may advance the production store, and the release cut is the single moment migrations are canonicalized.\n\n## Accounts and Homes become routable\n\nThe biggest shift is that \"which paid account an agent spends against\" and \"which machine hosts a Wave\" are no longer implicit. Accounts are the routing primitive (#1053): routes order provider accounts directly with a store-wide default, and Chrome profiles demote to mere login venues rather than run-time identity. A Wave's execution site becomes a user-owned address you can inspect and act on.\n\n- Give a Wave an execution address (`jack@local`, `ssh://jack@host`) and probe or idempotently start it through one API — `lf home probe|start` — surfaced as a per-card liveness chip with a contextual Open/Start action in the Mac app (#895, #918).\n- Connect managed Claude and Codex accounts via native OAuth with a browser/Chrome-profile handoff — no device-code paste — and import or adopt existing logins without re-auth (#921, #926, #943, #948, #959, #1023, #970).\n- Run any invocation as a chosen account with `lf --account <selector>` / `LF_ACCOUNT`, prefix-matched and live-verified; repeatable `--account claude=…` / `--only-account` grants flow over a lease broker (#1029, #1071).\n- Route each repo through an ordered default+backup account list, resolving and refreshing each credential once (#936, #1053), and auto-demote a strained account (limit window ≥75% used and not yet reset) below healthy ones before the provider refuses (#1061).\n- Forward account authority to a remote Home as a short-lived opaque lease over an owner-only reverse-forwarded socket — no credential files ever land on the remote, detached forms are rejected, and it fails closed (`docs/security.md`) (#1071).\n- `lfd`, a machine-level Home daemon, receives signed Linear webhooks into a durable, deduplicated delivery inbox; installable via launchd/systemd with bounded logs (#1012, #1049).\n\nNot yet live: `lf home`'s remote cold-start and attach transport shipped built and unit-tested but not verified against a live host (#895).\n\n## Tasks repair and recover themselves\n\nA Task now owns its full repair-and-recovery loop. When required checks go red on an open PR, the supervisor wakes a bounded ci-fix body, resumes that repair across a crash, settles it against the authoritative pushed head, and parks. When a body stalls, dies, or pins a lease, a live Project runner recovers it unattended and replay-safe. Completion is gated on real evidence, and every surface offers only actions the lifecycle can actually execute.\n\n**Autonomous ci-fix repair**\n- Persist a per-head CI reading so a waiting Task gets a CI-derived next move instead of sleeping through a failure, and keep an open PR CI-owned until fresh checks prove green before review handoff (#916, #1054, #1084).\n- Wake one bounded ci-fix generation on a fresh current-head failure, idempotent per (head, failing set), seeding the failing leaves rather than the required roll-up — never opening a PR or rotating the branch (#967).\n- Route wakes through a durable command ledger so a crash between \"body armed\" and \"incident stamped\" is recoverable, and settle exactly the wake the body was born for at turn exit, against a fresh GitHub read of the repaired head (#1024, #1026, #1018, #1063, #1070).\n- Refuse to arm on land-time preconditions only `lf pr land` can green, classify ci-fix infrastructure failures as Blocked, and count autonomy only when a serviced ci-fix command owned the repair (#1062, #1001, #1042, #1045).\n\n**Unattended recovery**\n- Recover abandoned work into one linked successor that adopts the worktree, directive, and PR history, carrying external Linear direction across succession and refusing unsafe worktree shapes before any ownership moves (#980, #1007, #994, #995, #1014).\n- Revoke dead leases on explicit resume for Tasks and Projects, release a lease stuck at `revoked` only on a fail-closed \"provably gone\" probe, recover stalled bodies replay-safely, redispatch stranded bodyless Sessions bounded by attempts, and self-heal worktree disk (#968, #973, #1055, #996, #1016, #1043).\n\n**Completion and PR authority**\n- Gate completion on settled PRs plus approved reviews, scoped to active epochs, withholding on committed follow-up past the merged tip while still settling over a proven-empty successor (#1015, #1060, #1050, #1056).\n- Prove Task PR range M==B parity end-to-end and refuse contamination before any push; reconcile out-of-band merges and reopened PRs (#924, #977, #989, #913, #928, #1032, #1046, #1059).\n- Derive one truthful legal-action model from total evidence, recommending only executable actions, and resolve caller authority as an explicit input (#1011, #1041, #1079).\n\n## Every Wave owns its Linear team\n\nThe shared `W2-*` namespace is dissolved: each Wave binds its own Linear team and identifier prefix (ENG-, SCI-, PRD-). The release ships the whole arc — binding, migrating existing issues, linking PRs back to their issue, streaming human edits inbound, and resolving completion from the issue's own owning team.\n\n- Bind a stable `pm.linear_team` in GOAL.md; `lf pm init --team-key` adopts or creates the team and steers only creation, leaving existing issues undisturbed (#899). The three real Waves' bindings landed (#1072).\n- `lf pm reteam` migrates older issues into the Wave team — dry-run by default, renumbering via Linear's stable issue UUID so Session/PR/comment links survive, moving Projects before their Issues and legacy issues before narrowing a Project (#902, #930, #1078). Ownership tightened to exactly one team, refusing to mutate while any Task body can still write the old identifier; `lf pm doctor` flags a Project stranded on a foreign team (#992, #907).\n- Opening/submitting/landing a Task PR writes an idempotent Linear attachment and managed comment without ever failing publication; degraded links surface in `lf task status` (#1010).\n- Verified Linear webhooks replace polling — human issue and comment edits become durable Task direction exactly once and wake the owning Session (#944).\n- `lf pm task done` resolves workflow state from the issue's own team (a Project can span teams), and `lf task reconcile` lets a Wave or Operator attest that an applied-but-unincorporated final directive was incorporated, settling a merged, Linear-complete Task with no new PR or provider turn (#1085, #1087, #1090).\n\n## One roadmap across CLI and Mac\n\nA machine-wide intent plane now spans CLI, Mac, and iOS from a single read. `lf roadmap --json` joins every Wave's durable plan to live execution evidence, buckets it in Rust, and stamps the sections on the wire so every surface projects identical Now / Available / Later views without re-deriving the rule.\n\n- `lf roadmap` ships the cross-Wave intent view and a shared Task-reference contract for multi-Task skills; Mac consumes it for all-Wave controls and a Now ⇄ Roadmap lens carrying PM completion, Session status, and liveness as three orthogonal facts (#900, #904, #911).\n- Task attention is projected once in Rust (one green/red/black/unknown signal with reason and legal controls) and consumed by Swift instead of a second state machine; the shared Wave/Project/Task lens folds attention upward and preserves last-good detail across refresh failures (#933, #963, #932).\n- Active Sessions lands as a new Control destination — a machine-wide census over `lf roadmap`, `lf runs`, and `lf handoff list --json` (#954).\n\n## Durable Sessions, disposable bodies\n\nA Session's durable identity — directive, worktree, PR history — is now cleanly separated from the disposable body that runs it, with an observation state model, fencing leases, successors, and honest interactive handoff.\n\n- `BodyObservation` is a derived projection (Working, Stalled, NeedsInput, Terminal…) over intent, liveness, and progress, wired onto the runtime snapshot with a real event-log progress signal (#898, #985).\n- Bodies are replaceable generations: `resume --model` hands off across providers keeping the Session, a monotonic write lease fences a single writer, and resumed bodies resolve the current Home lf rather than pinning the boot binary (#901, #903, #986).\n- Interactive handoff gains a durable shared-store contract, explicit wait-vs-defer flow policy, same-step resume on hand-back, a CLI exec adapter, remembered-surface resolution made honest so only exact-attach surfaces claim `.attach`, and IDE attach for known Claude sessions (#935, #941, #956, #961, #969, #978, #998).\n- `opencode serve` process trees spawn in their own group and are group-killed on stop, drop, signal, and at resident boot, ending orphan leaks (#1000).\n\n## Spend you can actually read\n\nUsage collapsed onto one grain: per-boundary deltas that readers sum instead of diff. `lf usage` now answers the subscription question — how much of each account's plan remains — with plans and window headers read from the provider, not hand-entered.\n\n- One spend grain: `run_events` become per-boundary deltas written at drain; migration `0.11.025` converts history (Claude totals preserved) and de-cumulates Codex turns that had inflated input to ~9.6B tokens (#1022).\n- Subscription-first `lf usage` over a new `provider_account_limits` table fed by harness rate-limit snapshots and on-demand Claude/Codex polling; revoked credentials surface `lf auth connect`; PLAN, SESSION USED, WEEKLY USED, and % TOKENS all read as provider truth (#1022, #1025).\n- `lf runs` / `lf status` report agent-backed skill runs with context, tokens, and cost; the raw process ledger moves to `lf execs` with `lf trace <exec-id>` (#906).\n- `lf top` graphs one-hour output-token throughput alongside live lf and provider processes via query-only SQLite (#938, #946).\n- opencode reports provider-measured tokens only when usage was actually reported (ending the zero-clobber), makes SSE hollow-body disconnects observable and recoverable, and a CI incident ledger with `lf ci` measures failed-PR recovery latency (#1036, #1020, #1021).\n- Ops telemetry routes to git-ignored `.lf/tmp/metrics/ops.jsonl` behind a path-contract guard, so read-only ops stop dirtying the checkout dispatch depends on (#982, #999).\n\n## Wave chat and review authority\n\nThe live wave stream went delta-granular, and wave chat learned to paint durable history first, then reconcile live. Review authority was re-grounded in inherited session identity rather than self-minted run ids.\n\n- Delta-granular wire: full `turn` frames re-baseline, small `turn-delta` frames carry each new item, and `resync` heals lag — O(fragment) instead of O(prose²) (#897).\n- Wave chat paints bounded durable history before endpoint discovery, reconciling live replay by stable turn id, with inline typed references and popovers, curated supervisor narration routed to conclusion + steps, and atomic journal delivery that truncates to a checkpoint on failure (#934, #947, #950, #990). Context Lab lets you inspect and edit the text shaping a Wave's sessions and spawn a real Project Task to refine it (#923).\n- Review authority is decided from inherited session markers (`LF_TASK/PROJECT_SESSION_ID`, `LF_WAVE_ID`), not a self-minted `LF_RUN_ID`, so humans can dispose reviews; a durable parent-dialogue protocol and attended `lf task review` gates ride on top (#1033, #1034, #951, #957).\n- Phase budgets are bounded with a split-off required `--ui-host` gate, and pre-land budget evidence persists under the git common dir with `--history` verdicts (#905, #917, #1057).\n- The docs and website were retold agent-first: README as landing page, reference material moved into `docs/`, and the operating contract shipped as a skill (#1069).\n\n## Operational notes\n\n**The promotion boundary.** v0.12.0 makes a hard rule out of a lesson learned twice: advancing `~/.lf/loopflow.db` past the installed binary's frontier is no longer a side effect of any ordinary command. Only `lf install promote` writes to the shared store, under a machine-global exclusive promotion lock that fences live Task and Project bodies; every other open is read/validate-only (#1086, #1077). Dev builds carry embedded provenance and resolve a per-worktree store under `~/.lf-dev/…` — they can no longer touch the production DB, and the `LF_ALLOW_PRODUCTION_DB_FROM_DEV` break-glass was removed entirely (#908, #964).\n\n- Gate every promotion on `lf install preflight [--json]`, a read-only verdict (`Promote` / `PromoteAndMigrate` / `Reject`) that fails closed on unreadable evidence and names every blocker — any live lease blocks replacement, leaving all targets untouched (#1074). `lf install promote` content-addresses the binary into `~/.lf/bin`, atomically repoints the CLI symlink, retains immutable rollback bytes, and activates before migrating; `install.py` now only stages artifacts and every global mutation (CLI swap, app bundle, helper, skills, rollback) routes through the Rust boundary (#1077, #1082, #1083).\n- **Migration authoring changed.** Author drafts ordinal-free with `new_migration.py <name>` and declare order via `--depends-on` — no git fetch, no rebase, no hand-assigned ordinals. Drafts are files named `<name>__<token>.sql` that touch neither the Rust registry nor git. Canonicalization happens once, at `lf release <version>`, which assigns contiguous ordinals and deterministic ids inside the release worktree so the generated files land in the release PR under real CI (#1076, #1081).\n- This release adds migrations `0.11.019`–`0.11.029`: task PR GitHub observation and Linear linkage, provider deliveries, Task Session successors, pruned capture state, CI incidents (and repaired-head), usage deltas, lineage boundary, and accounts-first. It also converges previously permuted live histories and can initialize an existing schema-less database (#940, #960, #962, #1035). A no-op migration open no longer takes an exclusive write lock that was killing five-second lease heartbeats (#1030).\n- **Upgrade path:** install v0.12.0 through an official release; only that install advances the store, and it does so under the drained-body promotion lock — never mid-turn.\n\n## Small changes\n\n- `lf` opens the desktop app by default (#931).\n- Bare `opencode` resolves to Loopflow's `opencode/glm-5.2` default, and Codex launches are forced to standard `service_tier=default` at the shared boundary (#965, #976).\n- `lf status` treats terminal Sessions for an absent Project as history, not a fatal error (#1002).\n- `lf wt list` is side-effect free (#912), and `lf wt ci --logs` resolves Actions run/job ids (#997).\n- `lf doctor` reports the running build's revision against merged main, plus provenance and the resolved DB path (#1065, #908).\n- `pull-local-bin` no longer breaks on empty-args expansion under macOS bash 3.2 (#929).\n- CI stopped caching Xcode DerivedData in `loopflow-ui-test` (#1037).\n\nThe Mac Trajectory/evidence memory section (#922) was reverted in full (#925); its conduct model was rejected before further work built on it.\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 203,
      "deletions": 22,
      "changed_files": 6
    }
